{"units": [{"id": "androdaktos-quote1", "translation": "attempts of man-slaying cleavers."}, {"id": "androdaktos-quote2", "translation": "hearing the man-slaying toil."}, {"id": "androthnes-orth1", "translation": "man-slaying"}, {"id": "androthnes-note1", "translation": "the destruction of Troy joined with the slaughter of men."}, {"id": "androthnes-quote1", "translation": "man-slaying destructions of Ilium"}, {"id": "androkmes-orth1", "translation": "man-slaying"}, {"id": "androkmes-def1", "translation": "man-slaying"}, {"id": "androkmes-quote1", "translation": "man-slaying axe."}, {"id": "androkmes-quote2", "translation": "with man-slaying toils."}, {"id": "androkmes-quote3", "translation": "man-slaying fortunes."}, {"id": "androkmes-quote4", "translation": "man-slaying destruction."}, {"id": "androktasia-orth1", "translation": "slaughter of men"}, {"id": "androktasia-def1", "translation": "slaughter of men"}, {"id": "androktasia-quote1", "translation": "to accomplish bitter-fruited slaughter of men with unlawful blood."}, {"id": "androktoneo-orth1", "translation": "to kill a man"}, {"id": "androktoneo-def1", "translation": "to kill a man"}, {"id": "androktoneo-quote1", "translation": "she killed my father, slaying a man."}, {"id": "andoleteira-orth1", "translation": "man-destroying"}, {"id": "andoleteira-def1", "translation": "man-destroying"}, {"id": "andoleteira-quote1", "translation": "man-destroying plague, shield-casting ruin."}, {"id": "andoleteira-quote2", "translation": "do not turn your wrath upon Helen, saying that she, a man-destroyer, wrought unbearable grief."}, {"id": "andropais-orth1", "translation": "young man"}, {"id": "andropais-def1", "translation": "young man"}, {"id": "andropais-quote1", "translation": "a man who is a boy."}, {"id": "andropletheia-orth1", "translation": "abundance of men"}, {"id": "andropletheia-def1", "translation": "abundance of men"}, {"id": "andropletheia-quote1", "translation": "abundance of men of the army."}, {"id": "andros-orth1", "translation": "Andros"}, {"id": "andros-def1", "translation": "island"}, {"id": "androsphageion-orth1", "translation": "place where a man is slain"}, {"id": "androsphageion-def1", "translation": "place where a man is slain"}, {"id": "androsphageion-note1", "translation": "where M has ἀνδρὸς σφάγιον [a man's victim] (σφαγεῖον [slaughter-house] pr.) καὶ πέδον [and the ground] (v made from p) ῥαντήριον [sprinkling]. Similar is ανδροκτονεῖον [man-slaying place], about which Phrynichus spoke in Bekker's Anecdota p. 28, 9."}, {"id": "androsphageion-quote1", "translation": "man-slaying place (thus Dobree) and ground-sprinkling,"}, {"id": "androsphageion-word1", "translation": "a man's victim (slaughter-house)"}, {"id": "androsphageion-word2", "translation": "and the ground"}, {"id": "androsphageion-word3", "translation": "sprinkling"}, {"id": "androsphageion-word4", "translation": "man-slaying place"}, {"id": "androtyches-orth1", "translation": "obtaining a husband"}, {"id": "androtyches-def1", "translation": "obtaining a husband"}, {"id": "androtyches-quote1", "translation": "lives of lovely maidens that obtain husbands."}, {"id": "androphontes-orth1", "translation": "man-slayer"}, {"id": "androphontes-def1", "translation": "man-slayer"}, {"id": "androphontes-quote1", "translation": "the man-slayer, the disturber of the city."}, {"id": "andron-orth1", "translation": "men's hall"}, {"id": "andron-def1", "translation": "men's hall"}, {"id": "andron-quote1", "translation": "through the well-tabled men's halls of my father."}, {"id": "andron-quote2", "translation": "into the hospitable men's halls of the house."}, {"id": "aneden-orth1", "translation": "without restraint"}, {"id": "aneden-def1", "translation": "without restraint (ἐκκεχυμένως [poured forth] Hesych.), i.e. without custom."}, {"id": "aneden-quote1", "translation": "to flee without restraint through the briny wave."}, {"id": "aneden-word1", "translation": "poured forth"}, {"id": "anekathen-orth1", "translation": "from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-def1", "translation": "from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-note1", "translation": "[where M has ἄνκαθεν [from above], with é written above by a corrector, i.e. έκαθεν [from there], so that ἄγκαθεν [from above] might seem to be written, which Hermann once conjectured, if ἂν [perhaps] could be absent. Therefore the letters καθεν [down], unless they are corrupt, are to be written καθ᾿ ἕν [one by one], which could be said for καθάπαξ [once for all].]"}, {"id": "anekathen-note2", "translation": "where the conjoined synonyms ἄνωθεν [from above] and ἀνέκαθεν [from above] are to be noted."}, {"id": "anekathen-note3", "translation": "Thus, with the meter demanding it, Pearson corrected what is in M, ἄγκαθεν [from above], with syncope, about which we have spoken under the word ἄγκαθεν [from above]."}, {"id": "anekathen-quote1", "translation": "[What could I say or do to meet you, having set my course one by one, where your bed holds you?]"}, {"id": "anekathen-quote2", "translation": "the frequent-rubbed stretchings of the hand from above, from above,"}, {"id": "anekathen-quote3", "translation": "from above I bring down the heavy-falling edge of my foot."}, {"id": "anekathen-word1", "translation": "M: from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-word2", "translation": "from there"}, {"id": "anekathen-word3", "translation": "from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-word4", "translation": "perhaps"}, {"id": "anekathen-word5", "translation": "down"}, {"id": "anekathen-word6", "translation": "one by one"}, {"id": "anekathen-word7", "translation": "once for all"}, {"id": "anekathen-word8", "translation": "from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-word9", "translation": "from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-word10", "translation": "M: from above"}, {"id": "anekathen-word11", "translation": "from above"}, {"id": "anektos-orth1", "translation": "bearable"}, {"id": "anektos-def1", "translation": "bearable"}, {"id": "anektos-quote1", "translation": "but it is not bearable."}, {"id": "aneleutheros-orth1", "translation": "unworthy of a free man"}, {"id": "aneleutheros-def1", "translation": "servile, unworthy of a free man"}, {"id": "aneleutheros-note1", "translation": "Which words, fabricated from verses 1494 and 1518, Seidler rightly deleted."}, {"id": "aneleutheros-quote1", "translation": "this unworthy bed."}, {"id": "aneleutheros-quote2", "translation": "[nor do I think that death was unworthy for him.]"}, {"id": "anellenostolos-orth1", "translation": "not dressed in Greek fashion"}, {"id": "anellenostolos-def1", "translation": "not dressed in Greek fashion"}, {"id": "anellenostolos-quote1", "translation": "What foreign crowd is this, not dressed in Greek fashion, that we address?"}, {"id": "anelpistos-orth1", "translation": "unhoped for"}, {"id": "anelpistos-def1", "translation": "unhoped for"}, {"id": "anelpistos-quote1", "translation": "this unhoped-for flight."}, {"id": "anemoeis-orth1", "translation": "windy"}, {"id": "anemoeis-def1", "translation": "windy"}, {"id": "anemoeis-note1", "translation": "where Blomfield rightly corrected κἀνεμόεντ᾽ ἂν [and windy would]."}, {"id": "anemoeis-quote1", "translation": "to tell the wrath of winged and ground-walking creatures and of windy aegises,"}, {"id": "anemoeis-word1", "translation": "and windy would"}, {"id": "anemos-orth1", "translation": "wind"}, {"id": "anemos-def1", "translation": "wind"}, {"id": "anemos-quote1", "translation": "of wild winds."}, {"id": "anemos-quote2", "translation": "the blasts of all winds."}, {"id": "anemos-quote3", "translation": "with rain-bearing winds."}, {"id": "anemos-quote4", "translation": "the blasts of winds."}, {"id": "anemos-quote5", "translation": "where neither does the wind allow to remain nor to sail out."}]}